---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import TechniquesReference from '../../components/TechniquesReference.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Prompt Engineering Techniques Reference" description="A quick reference guide to common prompt engineering techniques — zero-shot, few-shot, chain-of-thought, and more.">
  <TechniquesReference client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why technique choice depends on the task, not a universal "best"</h2>
  <p>Chain-of-thought helps with multi-step reasoning but adds unnecessary verbosity for simple lookups; few-shot examples improve consistency for pattern-matching tasks but cost extra tokens. Matching technique to task type produces better and more efficient results than defaulting to one approach everywhere.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify what's limiting your current prompt's output quality.</li>
    <li>Try the technique most likely to address that specific limitation.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Prompt Technique Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using chain-of-thought for simple, unambiguous tasks</strong>, adding unnecessary length and latency without accuracy benefit.</li>
    <li><strong>Providing too few or unrepresentative few-shot examples</strong>, which can bias the model toward matching surface patterns from the examples rather than the underlying task.</li>
    <li><strong>Not testing techniques against your actual use case</strong> — techniques that work well in general guidance don't always translate directly to every specific task.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Reference Prompt Techniques</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Improving accuracy on a reasoning-heavy task with chain-of-thought</li>
    <li>Getting more consistent output format with few-shot examples</li>
    <li>Learning the vocabulary of prompt engineering techniques to communicate more precisely with a team</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Prompt Engineering Techniques FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Which technique should I try first?</strong> Start simple with zero-shot and add complexity (few-shot, chain-of-thought) only if the simpler approach doesn't meet your accuracy or consistency needs.</p>
</ToolLayout>
